n_lift` vs `round <= n_lift`\n     - Evaluation mode: logup vs. zerocheck\n     - Size: batched by memory limit\n     - Complexity: DAG vs. monomial, monomial par-Y\n  3. Within each batch: **All AIRs in batch execute in single kernel launch**\n  4. Across batches: **Sequential batches with host syncs between**\n\n**Example Multi-AIR Batch (DAG evaluation):**\n- `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new()` at line 490 builds contexts for N traces\n- Each trace gets a `ZerocheckCtx` uploaded to device\n- Single `_zerocheck_batch_eval_mle()` kernel processes all N traces\n- Block partitioning: `num_blocks = sum(ceil(num_y_i / threads_per_block))` for all i in batch\n\n---\n\n### 7. Kernel Launch Configuration\n\n**For Batch Kernels:**\n- **Grid dimension:** `num_blocks` (cumulative blocks across all AIRs in batch)\n- **Block dimension:** `threads_per_block = min(max_num_y_in_batch, 128)`\n- **Intermediates:** Global per-AIR buffers allocated before kernel (lines 174-180, 320-330 in batch_mle.rs)\n- **Temp sums buffer:** One slot per block\n\n**For Monomial Batch Kernels:**\n- Similar grid/block configuration\n- **THREADS_PER_BLOCK:** 256 (different from DAG)\n\n**For Monomial Par-Y Kernels:**\n- Parallel over Y with chunk_size parameter\n- Better occupancy for high num_y traces\n\n---\n\n### 8. Potential Parallelization Points\n\nThe current implementation has **sequential batching** opportunities that could be parallelized:\n\n1. **Late traces (logup, monomial DAG):** Lines 1299-1338\n   - Currently sequential: logup first, then zerocheck\n   - **Could parallelize:** Launch both batches concurrently (different streams)\n\n2. **Early traces logup vs. zerocheck:** Lines 1341-1424\n   - Currently sequential: logup fully completes, then zerocheck\n   - **Could parallelize:** Logup and zerocheck batches on different streams\n\n3. **Memory-aware sub-batches:** Lines 446-499, 570-624 in batch_mle.rs\n   - Currently sequential per batch due to memory constraints\n   - **Could paralleli