prit, but that only drains the main stream and happens before Trace Commit, so it wouldn't explain this particular overhead.\n\nLet me trace through the actual timeline to see where the bottleneck really is. Looking at the timing breakdown, the 194ms input eval overhead appears to come from `current_stream_sync` waiting for prior GPU work to complete — the zero-fill operations, LogupZerocheckGpu initialization, and grinding phase all need to finish before worker threads can proceed. This isn't wasted overhead; it's real GPU work that has to happen regardless, just measured at a different point in the pipeline.\n\nThe potential CPU-GPU overlap is minimal since the CPU only spends about 5ms building work items and spawning threads while the GPU churns through 167ms of operations. The real puzzle is understanding where that 167ms of GPU work originates — whether it's from data uploads in the constructor or something else entirely.\n\nLooking at the actual code flow, the Trace Commit phase completes before the LogUp GKR phase starts, which means the constructor for LogupZerocheckGpu runs after Trace Commit finishes. This suggests there might be an opportunity to shift some of that constructor work to overlap with the Trace Commit phase instead of waiting until after it completes.\n\nNow I'm examining the timeline more carefully — there's about a 107ms gap between when Trace Commit finishes and when the input evaluation's first GPU kernel launches. During that window, the constructor is running on the CPU while queuing GPU operations, then the grinding step executes, and finally the input evaluation preparation and synchronization happen. I need to figure out whether that 167ms of GPU work is actual computation or if some of it is just idle time waiting for synchronization.\n\nLooking at the metrics, input_eval reports 266ms total but the actual kernel execution only spans 99ms, which suggests the sync operation is draining a significant backlog of queued GPU work from the constructor phase. If I trace back from when the first kernel starts at 5.107s and account for the 167ms sync time, the input_eval function itself probably started around 4.945s, which would help me understand the exact sequencing.\n\nNow I'm stepping back from these timing details to focus on the actual optimization opportunities. Looking at the breakdown across both segments, the fractional sumcheck is consuming the most time at 221ms total, followed by the round computations at 350ms and trace commit at 251ms, while the input eval overhead in segment 0 is particularly problematic at 167ms of sync time.\n\nThe most promising targets are increasing the multi-stream thread count from 4 to 8 threads—this could shave roughly 80ms off the round computations and another 47ms off input eval, though the segment 0 sync overhead will likely persist since it's tied to actual GPU constructor work. The fractional sumcheck and MLE rounds are harder to optimize without deeper algorithmic changes.\n\nBut looking at the bigger picture, the APC scaling isn't where we need it to be. Going from APC 0 to APC 300, the STARK time only improves by 1.38x when we're targeting 2x or better. The problem is that round 0, MLE rounds, and stacked reduction all get worse as AIR count increases, while only LogUp GKR scales down meaningfully. That's the real bottleneck I need to address.\n\nRound 0 is the worst offender at 350ms for APC 300. We're already using 4-stream multi-threading there, but the fundamental issue is that each AIR has a large trace height, so the GPU saturates on individual kernels and adding more parallelism doesn't help much. Even bumping to 8 streams might not move the needle significantly given how the workload distributes across segments. The clearest optimization is bumping the multi-stream thread count from 4 to 8 for Round 0 and GKR input eval—a straightforward constant change that should yield roughly 137ms total savings (8.7% improvement on STARK excluding trace).