{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EmfAUsxSn8GwxDgu34pyyr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Increase Multi-Stream Thread Count from 4 to 8\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tIncrease the number of OS threads (each with a per-thread CUDA stream via `cudaStreamPerThread`) from 4 to 8 for both Round 0 constraint evaluation and GKR input evaluation. At APC 300, ~310 AIRs per segment launch tiny kernels (1-4 SMs each) on the RTX 4090's 128 SMs. Nsys profiling shows 4 threads achieve only 1.7-2.1x effective parallelism (kernel time / wall time), leaving >90% of SMs idle. Doubling to 8 threads improves concurrent kernel occupancy, reduces chunk size for better load balance, and provides more overlap between GPU execution and per-thread CPU work.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Round 0 (`sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`)\n10\t\n11\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n12\t\n13\t- **Line 93:** `const NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS: usize = 4;`\n14\t- **Line 898:** `per_thread_temp_bytes = self.memory_limit_bytes / NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS.max(1);` — divides memory budget across threads\n15\t- **Line 937-941:** Thread count selection: `if work_items.len() >= 100 { NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS.min(work_items.len()) } else { 1 }`\n16\t- **Line 948:** `chunk_size = work_items.len().div_ceil(num_threads);` — divides AIRs into chunks\n17\t- **Lines 949-963:** `std::thread::scope` spawns worker threads, each calling `process_air_round0()` per AIR\n18\t\n19\t**Per-thread work** (`process_air_round0`, same file ~lines 1000-1100):\n20\tEach thread iterates its chunk of AIRs. Per AIR: launches constraint + interaction evaluation GPU kernels, performs D2H copy (`to_host_on_current_stream`), then CPU post-processing (transpose, iDFT, polynomial construction).\n21\t\n22\t**Current timing (APC 300):** Seg0=174ms, Seg1=176ms, total=350ms\n23\t\n24\t### GKR Input Eval (`log_gkr_input_evals`)\n25\t\n26\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n27\t\n28\t- **Line 30:** `const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;`\n29\t- **Line 258-262:** Thread count selection: same `>= 100` threshold\n30\t- **Lines 271-291:** `std::thread::scope` spawns workers, each calling `process_gkr_input_air()` per AIR\n31\t\n32\t**Per-thread work** (`process_gkr_input_air`, lines 105-209):\n33\tPer AIR: launches `logup_gkr_input_eval()` CUDA kernel, handles height lifting, D2H copy via `to_host_on_current_stream`.\n34\t\n35\t**Current timing (APC 300):** Seg0=266ms (includes 167ms stream-sync overhead from first-segment allocation), Seg1=72ms. Kernel wall spans: Seg0=99ms, Seg1=64ms.\n36\t\n37\t### Why current parallelism is low\n38\t\n39\tNsys data shows effective parallelism with 4 threads:\n40\t- Round 0: ~1.7x (350ms wall, ~600ms total kernel time across both segments)\n41\t- GKR input eval Seg1: 3.4x (64ms wall, 214ms kernel time)\n42\t- GKR input eval Seg0: 2.1x (99ms wall, 209ms kernel time)\n43\t\n44\tRoot causes:\n45\t1. **Chunk imbalance:** With 4 threads processing ~78 AIRs each (sorted by descending height), the first chunk has all the largest AIRs. The thread processing the tallest AIRs takes longest, creating a long tail.\n46\t2. **Memory pool mutex:** Every `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` call in `process_air_round0` and `process_gkr_input_air` acquires the global `MemoryManager` mutex. With 4 threads doing ~10 allocations per AIR, that's ~3,100 lock acquisitions per segment for Round 0.\n47\t3. **GPU underutilization:** At any instant, only 4 kernels are in-flight. Most kernels use 1-4 SMs, so 4-16 of 128 SMs are active (3-12%).\n48\t\n49\t## Changes\n50\t\n51\t### Change 1: Increase `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS` from 4 to 8\n52\t\n53\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, line 93\n54\t\n55\t**What:** Change `const NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS: usize = 4;` to `const NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS: usize = 8;`\n56\t\n57\t**Why:** With 8 threads, each processes ~39 AIRs instead of ~78. This reduces chunk imbalance (the tallest-AIR thread finishes sooner relative to others) and doubles the number of concurrent GPU kernels (8-32 SMs active vs 4-16).\n58\t\n59\t### Change 2: Increase `NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS` from 4 to 8\n60\t\n61\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, line 30\n62\t\n63\t**What:** Change `const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 4;` to `const NUM_GKR_INPUT_STREAMS: usize = 8;`\n64\t\n65\t**Why:** Same reasoning as Change 1. GKR input eval kernels average ~540μs each, small enough to benefit from more concurrent streams.\n66\t\n67\t### Change 3: (No code change needed) Memory budget division\n68\t\n69\tThe existing code at line 898 already divides memory budget by `NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS`:\n70\t```rust\n71\tlet per_thread_temp_bytes = self.memory_limit_bytes / NUM_ROUND0_STREAMS.max(1);\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\tWith 8 threads, each gets `memory_limit_bytes / 8` instead of `/4`. At APC 300, `memory_limit_bytes` is 5 GiB (the default), so each thread gets 640 MiB. This is sufficient for the intermediate buffers needed per-AIR (typically <100 MiB for the largest AIRs at APC 300, where heights are ≤16K).\n75\t\n76\tFor GKR input eval, there is no explicit memory budget division — each thread allocates per-AIR buffers independently. The per-AIR allocation size is small (a few MB), so 8 threads won't approach GPU memory limits.\n77\t\n78\t### No changes needed for threshold or fallback path\n79\t\n80\tThe existing `work_items.len() >= 100` threshold remains appropriate:\n81\t- APC 0: ~20 AIRs per segment → sequential path (unchanged)\n82\t- APC 100: ~120 AIRs per segment → 8 threads, 15 AIRs each (sufficient)\n83\t- APC 300: ~310 AIRs per segment → 8 threads, 39 AIRs each (optimal)\n84\t\n85\t## Invariants\n86\t\n87\t1. **Correctness:** Each thread writes to non-overlapping regions (Round 0: different `batch_sp_poly` entries via `trace_idx`; GKR input: different `leaves` offsets via `SendPtr`). Increasing thread count does not change the write pattern.\n88\t2. **APC 0 no regression:** The `>= 100` threshold means APC 0 (~20 AIRs/segment) always uses the sequential path, completely unaffected by the thread count constant.\n89\t3. **Memory safety:** Per-thread temp budget decreases from 1.25 GiB to 640 MiB. The largest AIR at APC 300 (height ~16K, ~100 columns) needs ~50 MiB of intermediates. 640 MiB is sufficient with >10x headroom.\n90\t4. **Stream isolation:** Each OS thread gets its own `cudaStreamPerThread`. Kernels on different streams are independent. The `current_stream_sync()` barrier before thread spawning ensures all prior GPU work is visible.\n91\t5. **Result ordering:** Round 0 results are identified by `trace_idx` and scattered in Phase 3. GKR input results are accumulated atomically into the leaves buffer. Neither depends on thread ordering.\n92\t\n93\t## Measurement Plan\n94\t\n95\tRun the standard benchmark suite:\n96\t\n97\t```bash\n98\tcd /home/georg/powdr && bash openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n99\t```\n100\t\n101\tThen analyze with:\n102\t```bash\n103\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json after_apc300\n104\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc100/metrics.json after_apc100\n105\tpython3 /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json after_apc000\n106\t```\n107\t\n108\t### Success criteria\n109\t\n110\t| Metric | Target | Measurement |\n111\t|--------|--------|-------------|\n112\t| Round 0 (APC 300) | < 300ms (>14% reduction from 350ms) | `spec.py` Round 0 line |\n113\t| LogUp GKR (APC 300) | < 530ms (>5.7% reduction from 562ms) | `spec.py` LogUp GKR line |\n114\t| STARK excl trace (APC 300) | < 1500ms (>4.8% reduction from 1576ms) | `spec.py` STARK excl trace line |\n115\t| STARK excl trace (APC 0) | < 2250ms (no regression) | `spec.py` STARK excl trace line |\n116\t| APC 100 Round 0 | < 250ms (improvement from 273ms) | `spec.py` Round 0 line |\n117\t\n118\t### Per-segment validation\n119\t\n120\tExtract per-segment metrics from `metrics.json`:\n121\t```python\n122\t# Check per-segment Round 0 and input_eval timing\n123\tprover.rap_constraints.round0_time_ms (seg 0 and seg 1)\n124\tprover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms (seg 0 and seg 1)\n125\t```\n126\t\n127\tVerify both segments show improvement (not just one).\n128\t\n129\t### Nsys validation\n130\t\n131\tRun nsys profiling for APC 300 and check:\n132\t1. Kernel concurrency: >8 concurrent kernel instances visible in the timeline during Round 0 and GKR input eval\n133\t2. Stream count: >8 distinct `streamId` values during the multi-threaded phases\n134\t3. Effective parallelism: total kernel time / wall span should be >3x (up from ~2x)\n135\t\n136\t## Rollback Criteria\n137\t\n138\tRevert if ANY of the following:\n139\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves less than 4% (<60ms reduction)\n140\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 3% (>65ms increase)\n141\t- Any correctness failure (prove+verify fails)\n142\t- GPU OOM error at any APC configuration\n143\t- APC 100 shows more than 5% regression in STARK excl trace\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A6ojr9DPp1tgy7dbuQbzdu","session_id":"e6440d5e-5fb7-4583-a464-0e3f9791a04a","uuid":"25fb06c3-2b6d-47f7-951d-b47dfb1869dc","timestamp":"2026-04-13T03:18:07.733Z"}
